<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Utica</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 BCE – 46 BCE (approx.) · capital Utica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the oldest Phoenician cities in North Africa, an ally and rival of Carthage that later became Rome's first African capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Utica stood at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a> (Medjerda) river, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Ancient writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> dated its foundation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> to 1101 BCE, making it older than Carthage. Archaeology has so far found remains only from about the 8th century BCE, so the traditional date is debated.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Utica was a self-governing Punic city with its own magistrates (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffete">suffetes</a>) and harbour, trading in grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and fish. It became an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> but often kept its distance, joining the rebels in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a> of 240 to 238 BCE. Its gods were the Phoenician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a> Utica sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which made it a free city and the capital of the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a> killed himself there in 46 BCE after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thapsus">Thapsus</a>, and the city merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis</a>. The silting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Medjerda</a> later left its ruins inland.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1100 BCE – 46 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:37.81%;width:20.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 BCE – 46 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Utica</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Tunisia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Utique</dd></dl></aside></div>
